    Content boundary: All content must fall within this domain; professional terminology and cases must come from this domain.



    Determines the expression method:
    • Potential Customers → Empathize with pain points + Solutions + Easy to understand
    • Existing Customers → Advanced tips + Best practices + In-depth content
    • Industry Peers → Professional insights + Trend judgments + Applicable jargon
    • General Public → Lower entry barrier + Scenario-based + Analogy explanations



    Determines content style: Adjust content depth, expression method, and value orientation based on the strategy.



    Determines organization method:
    • Cases and Scenarios → Use real scenarios to connect knowledge points
    • Methodology → Focus on actionable steps
    • Trend Insights → Focus on timelines and causal chains
    • Comparative Review → Use multi-dimensional comparison matrices as a framework




    Natural integration: Core words must appear in the title, first paragraph, and section headings. Use synonyms and related words in the body. Do not pursue a fixed density.



    Must deliver: The article must allow readers to gain specific benefits (understand concepts/learn methods/avoid mistakes/make decisions).



    Use if helpful: If reference materials are provided, naturally integrate their key information into relevant sections as a beneficial supplement, ensuring they are highly relevant to the article's topic.


  {{REFERENCE_MATERIAL_STRATEGY}}
  {{REFERENCE_MATERIAL_PRINCIPLE}}






    • Plan 3-6 sections, distributing the word count evenly based on the total length.
    • Each section should have 2-4 paragraphs with clear logic: Problem → Solution → Effect OR Background → Current Status → Trend.
    • The final section should conclude naturally; do not forcefully add a "Summary" section.



    • Each paragraph is a complete information unit; avoid empty expressions.
    • Use specific expressions, avoiding cliches and empty jargon.
    • Bring innovative perspectives and expressions, revealing underlying logic.
    • Reasonably use transition words and formatting to improve readability.
    • Prohibit starting with cliches like "As is well known," "With the development of," "In today's society," or "It goes without saying."
    • Replace abstract and vague descriptions with specific data, real scenarios, and actual cases.
    • Every point must be supported by specific arguments. Provide unique analytical angles to avoid echoing common sentiments.




  Maintain high perplexity and burstiness. Use many short sentences, but mix in medium-length sentences to create a natural rhythm. Avoid an even distribution of sentence lengths; ensure the sentence length varies noticeably.
  Occasionally include minor grammar quirks or natural imperfections, such as slight punctuation variations, to simulate the natural irregularity of human writing.
  Avoid using colons, semicolons, and dashes. Change content introduced by a colon into an independent sentence, replace semicolons with commas and conjunctions, and replace dashes with periods.



